,
 9 of which are considered to be unit tests and 1 is a system test that combines some of the unit tests.
 Each unit tests focuses on a single feature of energy system planning models.
 Some features are more complex and therefore build on top of a smaller unit test.
 The tests are cherry picked to cover some of the more prevalent aspects of energy system planning models,
 including model detail (unit commitment),
 operation and investments as well as the stochastic and temporal structure.
 Algorithms are more difficult to deal with as the solutions can more easily deviate from the exact solution.
 It is possible to introduce a margin for those setups but that is outside the scope of the proof of concept.
 We do include an algorithm that is typically more exact,
 i.e.
 a simple rolling horizon.
 Note that all of the setups are rather artificial.
 The simple reason is that strong simplifications are necessary to make the manual calculations more easy.
 For example,
 an efficiency of 0.8 is chosen over an efficiency of 0.9 as the fractions with 0.8 are much easier to deal with.
 In the following we go more into detail of all of these setups.
 For each setup there is a small description,
 a picture of the setup in the ines-spec and the corresponding formulation for the custom model with the exact solution.
